WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.040
If you're building a computer vision application, rather than training the weights from scratch,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.880
from random initialization, you often make much faster progress if you download weights

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.640
that someone else has already trained on a network architecture and use that as pre-training

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.800
and transfer that to a new task that you might be interested in.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.920
The computer vision research community has been pretty good at posting lots of datasets

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.920
on the internet.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.600
If you hear of things like ImageNet or MS-CoCo or Pascal types of datasets, these are the

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.280
names of different datasets that people have posted online and that a lot of computer vision

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.280
researchers have trained their algorithms on.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.760
Sometimes this training takes several weeks and might take many, many GPUs and the fact

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.200
that someone else has done this and gone through the painful hyperparameter search process

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.200
means that you can often download open source weights that took someone else many weeks

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.520
or months to figure out and use that as a very good initialization for your own neural

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:06.480
network and use transfer learning to transfer knowledge from some of these very large public

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.240
datasets to your own problem.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.880
Let's take a deeper look at how to do this.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.080
Let's start with an example.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.000
Let's say you're building a cat detector to recognize your own pet cat.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:34.700
According to the internet, Tigger is a common cat name and Misty is another common cat name.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:41.620
Let's say your cats are called Tigger and Misty and there's also neither.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.620
So you have a classification problem with three classes.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.620
Is this picture Tigger or is it Misty or is it neither?

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:54.060
And we'll ignore the case of both of your cats appearing in a picture.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.620
Now you probably don't have a lot of pictures of Tigger or Misty so your training set will

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:00.900
be small.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.260
So what can you do?

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:08.620
I recommend you go online and download some open source implementation of a neural network

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.980
and download not just the code but also the weights.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.900
There are a lot of networks you can download that have been trained on for example the ImageNet

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.420
dataset which has a thousand different classes.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:31.380
So the network might have a softmax unit that outputs one of a thousand possible classes.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:40.140
What you can do is then get rid of the softmax layer and create your own softmax unit that

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:46.660
outputs Tigger or Misty or neither.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:53.660
And in terms of the network I'd encourage you to think of all of these layers as frozen.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:03:00.260
So you freeze the parameters in all of these layers of the network and you would then just

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:08.180
train the parameters associated with your softmax layer which is a softmax layer with three possible

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.460
outputs, Tigger, Misty or neither.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:18.460
And by using someone else's pre-trained weights you might be able to get pretty good performance

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.780
on this even with a small dataset.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:28.220
Fortunately, a lot of deep learning frameworks support this mode of operation and in fact

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:35.140
depending on the framework it might have things like trainable parameter equals zero.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:40.140
You might set that for some of these earlier layers in order to just say don't train those

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:47.620
weights or sometimes you have a parameter like freeze equals one and these are different

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.060
weights and different deep learning programming frameworks that let you specify whether or

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.500
not to train the weights associated with a particular layer.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.660
And so in this case you would train only the softmax layers weights that freeze all

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.380
of the earlier layers weights.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:10.820
One other neat trick that may help for some implementations is that because all of these

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.420
earlier layers are frozen there is some fixed function that doesn't change because you're

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:21.060
not changing it, you're not training it that takes this input image X and maps it to

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.460
some set of activations in that layer.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:31.140
So one other trick that could speed up training is you just pre-compute that layer, the

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:37.020
features, the activations from that layer and just save them to disk.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.340
And what you're doing is you're using this fixed function in this first part of the neural

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:49.420
network to take as input any input, any image X and compute some feature vector for it and

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:56.580
then you're training a shallow softmax model from this feature vector to make a prediction.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:03.580
And so one step that could help your computation is you just pre-compute that layer's activation

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.420
for all the examples in your training set and save them to disk and then just train a softmax

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.740
classifier on top of that.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.500
So the advantage of the save to disk or the pre-compute method, the save to disk method

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:20.500
is that you don't need to recompute those activations every time you take an epoch

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.380
or take a pass through a training set.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:29.020
So this is what you do if you have a pretty small training set for your task.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.500
What even the larger training set?

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.500
So one rule of thumb is if you have a larger label data set, so maybe you just have a ton

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:42.300
of pictures of Tigger, Misty, as well as I guess pictures of neither of them, one thing

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:46.100
you could do is then freeze fewer layers.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:53.540
So maybe you freeze just these layers and then train these later layers.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.820
Although if the output layer has different classes then you need to have your own output

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.500
unit anyway.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.700
Tigger, Misty, or Neither.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.780
And there are a couple ways to do this.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:13.620
You could take the last few layers and there are a couple ways to do this.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.060
You could take the last few layers weights and just use that as initialization and do

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.660
gradient descent from there.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:25.860
Or you could also blow away these last few layers and just use your own new hidden units

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.140
and then your own final softmax output.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.340
So either of these methods could be worth trying.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.740
But maybe one pattern is if you have more data, the number of layers you freeze could

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:44.020
be smaller and then the number of layers you train on top could be greater.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:48.140
And the idea is that if you have a bigger data set then maybe you have enough data not just

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:53.980
to train a single softmax unit but to train some, you know, model size neural network

00:06:53.980 --> 00:07:01.580
that comprises the last few layers of this final network that you end up using.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:06.740
And then finally if you have a lot of data, one thing you might do is take this open

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:14.580
source network and weights and use the whole thing just as initialization and train the

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.580
whole network.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:21.060
Although again, if this was a thousand-note softmax and you have just three outputs,

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.580
you need your own softmax output to output the labels you care about.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:32.220
But the more label data you have for your task, so the more pictures you have of Tigger,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.940
Amnesty, and neither, the more layers you could train.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.260
And in the extreme case, you could use the weights you download just as initialization

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:47.260
so they would replace random initialization and then you could do gradient descent training,

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:51.060
updating all the weights and all the layers of the network.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.860
So that's transfer learning for the training of confidence.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:08:00.060
In practice, because the open data sets on the Internet are so big and the weights you

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.700
can download that someone else has spent weeks training, has learned from so much data, you

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:11.300
find that for a lot of computer vision applications, you just do much better if you download someone

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:16.300
else's open source weights and use that as initialization for your problem.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:21.900
So in all the different disciplines and all the different applications of deep learning,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.580
I think that computer vision is one where transfer learning is something that you should

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:32.060
almost always do unless you actually have a very, very large, unless you have an exceptionally

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:35.740
large data set to train everything else from scratch yourself.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:41.260
But transfer learning is just very worth seriously considering unless you have an exceptionally

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:46.580
large data set and a very large computational budget to train everything from scratch by

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:47.060
yourself.
